{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering the master transient threshold to 40 essentially removes pre-echo in the replicated band: the detector then notices the attacks it was previously missing and gives them their own envelope instead of averaging across the transient. The effect is monotonic, reproducible at both bitrates, and saturates below 60 - so {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 40} is the recommended starting point on percussive material. Everything from 100 upwards is byte-identical to stock, which also confirms the knob is cleanly opt-in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 NOTE: {\f1 --sbr-start}/{\f1 --sbr-stop} are validated BY FDK \u8212- an incorrect start/stop COMBINATION (wrong number of master bands) will give "encoder initialization failed". This is a limitation of SBR itself, not a bug. Choose pairs (e.g. for 64k stereo start=5 stop=9, start=8 stop=14 work).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 4. Parametric Stereo (HE-AAC v2)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PS describes stereo with a few parameters (IID/ICC...). Here you can control them, even at the cost of the stereo image.\par}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx2160\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx4320\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx6480\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx8640
