\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The interesting result is that requesting 4 envelopes alone changes nothing \u8212- stock output and {\f1 --ps-env 4} are byte-identical, because the automatic halving loop immediately collapses them again. The gain only appears once {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} stops that loop: a 34 % reduction in panorama error for roughly the same file size. If you only take one thing from this group, take {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 NOTE about IPD/OPD: FDK codes IID (loudness differences) and ICC (coherence) only. The interchannel {\i phase} parameters are not emitted \u8212- {\f1 ps_encode.cpp} literally writes zeros and comments {\f1 "IPD OPD not supported right now"}. Note however that the encoder already computes both the real and the imaginary part of the L/R cross-spectrum ({\f1 pwrCr} / {\f1 pwrCi}) and currently uses only their magnitude, so the phase information is present but discarded; the Huffman tables and bitstream writers for IPD/OPD also already exist in {\f1 ps_bitenc.cpp}.\par}
