{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --afterburner <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 0/1 (also stock {\f1 -a})\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 1\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Afterburner (more precise quantization).\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IMPORTANT about PNS at low bitrate: FDK has a tuning table ({\f1 levelTable}) that COMPLETELY disables PNS below ~28 kbps (the bitrate row 0-27999 = all zeros for every samplerate). That is why at 24 kbps {\f1 --pns}/{\f1 --pns-start} do NOTHING (the audio sounds "like MP3/MDCT"), while at 64 kbps the difference is large. {\f1 --force-pns} bypasses this gate (uses the first active row of the table), so PNS also works at 24k. FDK limitation: PNS still requires TNS enabled and a non-VBR mode \u8212- otherwise it is zeroed higher up in the chain (we can't do anything about it without a deeper rebuild).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6. Masking / ATH\par}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx2160\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx4320\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx6480\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Switch\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Values\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Default\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Description\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ath-scale <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 1..~4096 (Q8)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 256\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Masking threshold scale in Q8 (256 = x1.0). {\f1 >256} raises the thresholds (more noise, fewer bits per band), {\f1 <256} lowers them (cleaner, more bits). Works in FDK's ld64 domain as an additive log2 offset. NOTE: this only touches the log-domain threshold copy and is partly undone downstream by the min-SNR / 29 dB clamps \u8212- for a stronger, more direct effect prefer {\f1 --minsnr-scale} below.\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --spread-mask <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Q8, >=0\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 -1 (off)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Scales the spreading of masking between bands. {\f1 <256} = less masking = more detail. Biggest effect where bits are limited (96-192k).\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --minsnr-scale <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 1..no limit (Q8)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 -1 (off)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 MusePack-style: scales the REQUIRED per-band coding SNR ({\f1 sfbMinSnrLdData}, FDK's closest thing to TMN/NMT). {\f1 <256} = demand HIGHER SNR = more detail/bits; {\f1 >256} = coarser. More effective than {\f1 --ath-scale} because min-SNR is what the avoid-holes logic clamps thresholds back to. 256=off.\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 1..no limit (Q8)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 -1 (off)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Scales FDK's MAX_SNR ceiling (~\u8722 ?1 dB). {\f1 >256} lets bands demand more than the stock cap. 256=off.\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 1..no limit (Q8)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 -1 (off)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Scales FDK's MIN_SNR floor (~\u8722 ?25 dB). 256=off.\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --reduce-clamp <0|1>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 0, 1\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 1 (on)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 0} drops the "29 dB Ratio" threshold-reduction ceiling in the CBR quantizer, letting thresholds be pushed deeper (more bits into demanding bands). Pairs with {\f1 --minsnr-scale} for extreme detail. CBR only (VBR uses a different path).\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What really helps at low and medium bitrate (10-144 kbps)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A common question: can you still squeeze something out of coding accuracy/efficiency (Huffman, quantization iterations, etc.)? The honest answer after reviewing the FDK code:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Huffman coding (section merging, codebook selection in {\f1 dyn_bits.cpp}) is already optimal (greedy section merging giving min. bits). There is no meaningful knob there \u8212- and exposing it would only worsen the result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The quantization iteration loop ({\f1 maxIterations}) is a RESCUE mechanism for bit shortage; increasing it does nothing (details in the manual, chapter 9a).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Internal thresholds (minSnr adaptation, {\f1 bits2PeFactor}) are fixed-point arithmetic with hard ranges \u8212- moving them risks instability, not improvement.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The REAL set of quality levers for 10-144 kbps is ALREADY exposed:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --ath-scale <256} \u8212- globally lower the masking threshold (more detail for bits).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --spread-mask <256} \u8212- less inter-band masking (more bands coded).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --ms-precision >256} \u8212- shallower holes in the MS bands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --is-aggression} \u8212- control intensity stereo (crucial at low bitrate).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --force-pns} + {\f1 --pns-start} \u8212- noise control at very low bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab under HE-AAC: {\f1 --sbr-invf}, {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset}, {\f1 --speech} (speech).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not missing functionality \u8212- these are the same levers that professional tuning uses, just manually. Start with {\f1 --ath-scale} and {\f1 --spread-mask}, one at a time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 7. Bias of short/long block switching (any profile)\par}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx2160\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx4320\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx6480\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Switch\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Values\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Default\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Description\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --block-bias <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 0..255\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 -1 (off)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Shifts the short/long decision threshold. 128 = encoder default (no change), >128 favors short blocks (more "transient"), <128 favors long ones, 0 = practically only long.\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IMPORTANT: {\f1 --block-bias} always produces a standard-compliant stream (it shifts the attack-detection threshold, it doesn't forcibly impose a block type). It replaced the old {\f1 --allshort}/{\f1 --alllong}, which created an ILLEGAL stream (hard forcing of the short window without recomputing SFB/grouping -> decoders rejected it, Winamp "skipped like on a scratched disc"). If you want maximum long blocks: {\f1 --block-bias 0}; maximum short: {\f1 --block-bias 255}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 8. MS decision bias (honestly: a tool with a WEAK effect)\par}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx2160\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx4320\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx6480\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Switch\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Values\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Default\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Description\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ms-bias <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 0..255 (Q8)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 -1 (off)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Shifts the L/R vs MS decision threshold. Q8, 128 = +0.5 in FDK's ld64 units. >0 = MS more eager. Reacts already from ~32 (after scale recalibration).\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HONESTLY about {\f1 --ms-bias} \u8212- this is the weakest tool in the whole set, and now we know why "it does little". MS (mid/side) is a LOSSLESS transform: mid=L+R, side=L-R reconstructs exactly back to L/R. Enabling/disabling MS on a given band does NOT change the sound \u8212- it only changes HOW MANY BITS the encoding takes. The encoder chooses a near-optimal decision per band anyway; {\f1 --ms-bias} only shifts a few BORDERLINE bands. Measurement (L/R correlation after decoding, ADTS size): effect on the order of <0.1% of the size and correlation changes in the 4th decimal place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Technical note: in the previous version the bias scale was ~256x too weak (multiplier <<15 instead of <<23) \u8212- hence "128 did nothing, only 2048 moved something". Now 128 = a real +0.5 ld64 as in the description, so it reacts from ~32. But even a correctly scaled bias inherently has a small impact (see above).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Want to REALLY control MS? Use the hard switches, not the bias:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --msmask 0} \u8212- DISABLE MS entirely (pure, independent L/R). This is the right choice for center-cancel / vocal removal (zero channel mixing by the coder).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --msmask 1} \u8212- force MS on all bands (max bit economy).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --msbands} / {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi} \u8212- restrict MS to selected bands. Measurement: msmask 0 vs 1 gives ~900 B difference on a 2s sample; ms-bias only ~2 B.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 9. Quasi-constrained VBR (CBR engine + wider breathing)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IMPORTANT: without these switches, CBR is 100% UNCHANGED (verified: bit-identical to the original binary). You enable them deliberately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How AAC breathes: even in CBR, frames borrow from the bit-reservoir, so one frame can be ~122 kbps and the next ~141, as long as the average = target. These knobs widen/narrow that breathing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HARD CEILING for everything: an AAC frame holds MAX 6144 bits per channel (=768 bytes/channel); stereo => 12288 bits/frame. At 44100 Hz one frame = 1024 samples = ~23.2 ms, so bits/frame = kbps * 23.22. (E.g. 128k stereo: average ~2972 bits/frame; ceiling 12288.)\par}
{
\trowd \trgaph120
\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx2160\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx4320\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx6480\clbrdrb\brdrs\cellx8640
